Consider the following examples:

<details>

<summary>Test code</summary>

```c#
using System.Diagnostics;

using var client = new HttpClient(new MyHandler(contentLength: int.MaxValue + 1L));

await Test("Content.ReadAsStringAsync", async () =>
{
    using var response = await client.SendAsync(new HttpRequestMessage(HttpMethod.Get, "http://foo"), HttpCompletionOption.ResponseHeadersRead);
    await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
});

await Test("Content.ReadAsByteArrayAsync", async () =>
{
    using var response = await client.SendAsync(new HttpRequestMessage(HttpMethod.Get, "http://foo"), HttpCompletionOption.ResponseHeadersRead);
    await response.Content.ReadAsByteArrayAsync();
});

await Test("GetAsync", async () =>
{
    await client.GetAsync("http://foo");
});

await Test("GetStringAsync", async () =>
{
    await client.GetStringAsync("http://foo");
});

await Test("GetByteArrayAsync", async () =>
{
    await client.GetByteArrayAsync("http://foo");
});

static async Task Test(string name, Func<Task> test)
{
    Console.WriteLine(name);

    Stopwatch s = Stopwatch.StartNew();
    Exception? exception = null;
    try
    {
        await test();
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        exception = ex;
    }
    s.Stop();

    Console.WriteLine($"[{(int)s.ElapsedMilliseconds,4} ms] {exception?.Message ?? "No error"}");
    Console.WriteLine();
}

public sealed class MyHandler : HttpMessageHandler
{
    private readonly long _contentLength;

    public MyHandler(long contentLength)
    {
        _contentLength = contentLength;
    }

    protected override HttpResponseMessage Send(HttpRequestMessage request, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        var response = new HttpResponseMessage();

        response.Content = new StreamContent(new InfiniteStream());
        response.Content.Headers.ContentLength = _contentLength;

        return response;
    }

    protected override Task<HttpResponseMessage> SendAsync(HttpRequestMessage request, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        return Task.FromResult(Send(request, cancellationToken));
    }

    private sealed class InfiniteStream : Stream
    {
        private bool _readIssued;

        public override ValueTask<int> ReadAsync(Memory<byte> buffer, CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
        {
            if (!_readIssued)
            {
                _readIssued = true;
                Console.WriteLine("Reading content stream");
            }

            buffer.Span.Fill((byte)'a');
            return new ValueTask<int>(buffer.Length);
        }

        public override void Flush() => throw new NotImplementedException();
        public override int Read(byte[] buffer, int offset, int count) => throw new NotImplementedException();
        public override long Seek(long offset, SeekOrigin origin) => throw new NotImplementedException();
        public override void SetLength(long value) => throw new NotImplementedException();
        public override void Write(byte[] buffer, int offset, int count) => throw new NotImplementedException();
        public override bool CanRead => true;
        public override bool CanSeek => false;
        public override bool CanWrite => throw new NotImplementedException();
        public override long Length => throw new NotImplementedException();
        public override long Position { get => throw new NotImplementedException(); set => throw new NotImplementedException(); }
    }
}
```

</details>

The code will print something along the lines of:
```
Content.ReadAsStringAsync
[   1 ms] Cannot write more bytes to the buffer than the configured maximum buffer size: 2147483647.

Content.ReadAsByteArrayAsync
[   0 ms] Cannot write more bytes to the buffer than the configured maximum buffer size: 2147483647.

GetAsync
[   0 ms] Cannot write more bytes to the buffer than the configured maximum buffer size: 2147483647.

GetStringAsync
Reading content stream
[1530 ms] Cannot write more bytes to the buffer than the configured maximum buffer size: 2147483647.

GetByteArrayAsync
[   1 ms] Capacity must be positive. (Parameter 'capacity')
```


The first few variants immediately check the `Content-Length` and throw if it's higher than the `MaxResponseContentBufferSize` limit.

The odd cases are:
- `GetStringAsync` where we [cast the header value to `int` and overflow](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/36aa98bfc5fa80fda2cefbbe2140cdef2e9ac000/src/libraries/System.Net.Http/src/System/Net/Http/HttpClient.cs#L205), but the [`LimitArrayPoolWriteStream`'s ctor isn't guarding against negative values](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/0ff1f89fe316157011b6714e4312b5f4e65e146f/src/libraries/System.Net.Http/src/System/Net/Http/HttpContent.cs#L931-L940).
  - As a result, we'll attempt to read enough content to hit the limit and then throw.
- `GetByteArrayAsync` where we [cast the header value to `int` and overflow](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/36aa98bfc5fa80fda2cefbbe2140cdef2e9ac000/src/libraries/System.Net.Http/src/System/Net/Http/HttpClient.cs#L286), causing `MemoryStream`'s ctor to throw.
  - As a result, we'll propagate the wrong exception to the user.

---

Now change the example to enforce a 100 byte length instead of the default 2 GB:
```c#
using var client = new HttpClient(new MyHandler(contentLength: 101));
client.MaxResponseContentBufferSize = 100;
```

```
Content.ReadAsStringAsync
Reading content stream
[1497 ms] Cannot write more bytes to the buffer than the configured maximum buffer size: 2147483647.

Content.ReadAsByteArrayAsync
Reading content stream
[1681 ms] Cannot write more bytes to the buffer than the configured maximum buffer size: 2147483647.

GetAsync
[   0 ms] Cannot write more bytes to the buffer than the configured maximum buffer size: 100.

GetStringAsync
Reading content stream
[   1 ms] Cannot write more bytes to the buffer than the configured maximum buffer size: 100.

GetByteArrayAsync
Reading content stream
[   0 ms] Cannot write more bytes to the buffer than the configured maximum buffer size: 100.
```

`Content.ReadAs` helpers don't enforce `HttpClient`'s `MaxResponseContentBufferSize` but default back to 2 GB since we're using `ResponseHeadersRead`.

Now the odd case is:
- `GetAsync` (buffered `SendAsync`) will immediately validate the header length, but `GetString/ByteArray` helpers will read the content until the limit.

We should fix the overflows and unify the behavior across these methods (likely to always check the header up front).

